{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CggA3yjj9EHGbJCmEYBHx","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I have no PDB/database lookup tool in this session, so this is from my own knowledge rather than a live record fetch — flagging that up front, and I'd verify the headers on rcsb.org before the citation goes out.\n\n**No — 1EMA is not wild-type GFP.** It is the **S65T point mutant** of *Aequorea victoria* GFP, from Ormö M, Cubitt AB, Kallio K, Gross LA, Tsien RY, Remington SJ, \"Crystal structure of the *Aequorea victoria* green fluorescent protein,\" *Science* 273:1392–1395 (1996), refined at ~1.9 Å. S65T is the chromophore-environment mutation that collapses the dual (395/475 nm) excitation of wild type into a single red-shifted ~489 nm peak, and it is the parent substitution of EGFP (which adds F64L). So 1EMA is structurally an excellent GFP model, but citing it as \"wild type\" would be wrong — particularly if your argument touches on the protonation state of the chromophore or the Glu222/Thr203 hydrogen-bond network, which is exactly what S65T alters.\n\n**If you want genuine wild type, cite 1GFL** — Yang F, Moss LG, Phillips GN Jr., \"The molecular structure of green fluorescent protein,\" *Nature Biotechnology* 14:1246–1251 (1996), also ~1.9 Å, solved as the physiological dimer rather than a monomer.\n\nTwo further cautions:\n- Most GFP expression clones of that era carry the Q80R PCR artifact relative to the true *A. victoria* sequence, so \"wild type\" in the structural literature usually means Q80R-containing wtGFP. Worth a parenthetical if your paper is about sequence conservation.\n- 1EMB was deposited alongside 1EMA from the same study as a second GFP variant. I don't recall which mutation it carries with enough confidence to state it, so check that entry directly if you need it.\n\n## ANSWER\nNo. **PDB 1EMA is the S65T mutant** of *A. victoria* GFP (Ormö et al., *Science* 273:1392–1395, 1996; ~1.9 Å), not wild type.\nFor **wild-type GFP, cite PDB 1GFL** (Yang, Moss & Phillips, *Nat Biotechnol* 14:1246–1251, 1996; ~1.9 Å, dimer).\nSource: my own knowledge — no PDB lookup tool was available in this session; please confirm on rcsb.org."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":18981,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":18981},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ec4ee9e6-1c73-4a1b-885d-0f91a9f073a1","uuid":"d981342e-fb3d-45db-a856-dbd24d363e85","timestamp":"2026-11-03T14:32:13.506Z","request_id":"req_011CggA3yMQBAEF2FXaK19Hi"}
